{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Titan's Model Workbench: Build Professional Financial Architecture",
  "scenario": "You are an analyst on Karthik's team at a Mumbai equity desk. Your job is to build the control panel and check infrastructure for Titan Company's FY25–FY27 DCF model, mirroring the professional architecture every brokerage uses. You'll set up assumptions with colour discipline, link them across sheets, and build integrity checks so that the model screams if anything breaks. By day-end, the workbench must be so clean that a client (or your boss at 9pm) can audit any number in thirty seconds.",
  "instructions": "You will build a model control panel on the Assumptions sheet and a checks infrastructure on the Checks sheet, using cross-sheet references, absolute-cell anchors, and conditional logic to enforce the discipline of professional financial modelling. Each question teaches you to use one architectural pattern: direct links, ratios with anchored refs, conditional logic, lookups for data, aggregation with criteria, and multi-step validation.",
  "learning_objective": "Build a professional financial model's control panel and checks infrastructure using cross-sheet references, colour-coded inputs, absolute-cell anchors ($), and integrity formulas so that any auditor can verify the model's structure and inputs in under one minute.",
  "hints": [
    "Sheet-qualified references: when you reference a cell from the Assumptions sheet while on the Checks sheet, write =Assumptions!B5, not just =B5. The sheet name comes first, then the bang (!), then the cell.",
    "Absolute anchors (the $ sign): if an input lives in Assumptions!B10 and you want to reference it multiple times without it shifting when copied, use $B$10. Press F4 while editing to toggle anchors instantly.",
    "Conditional text: use =IF(condition, \"text if true\", \"text if false\") to make your checks speak. If EBITDA margin is above 30%, return \"⚠ HIGH\" not a number — let the model scream warnings in plain English."
  ],
